<!-- ============ WHY DEMOS PROVE NOTHING ============ -->
<section class="ac-blue sunk section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">The Problem With Demos</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">A demo is a performance of the easy case.</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">It runs on a clean network, a seeded catalog, an empty store and an operator who has used the product for years. Your stores have none of those things. The gap between the two is where an implementation goes wrong, and it is invisible unless you design for it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="intx-grid intx-grid-3">
      <div class="intx-item">
        <span class="intx-n">01</span>
        <h3>Ask for the failure, not the feature</h3>
        <p>Anyone can show you a sale completing. Ask to see one interrupted. The recovery path is the product; the happy path is the brochure.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="intx-item">
        <span class="intx-n">02</span>
        <h3>Use your data, not theirs</h3>
        <p>A seeded catalog hides everything that makes yours hard: size and width matrices, variants that differ by market, the SKUs nobody has cleaned up since 2019.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="intx-item">
        <span class="intx-n">03</span>
        <h3>Put it in an untrained pair of hands</h3>
        <p>The person demonstrating has a thousand hours on the product. Your seasonal hire in November will have twenty minutes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ THE SIX TESTS ============ -->
<section class="section" id="tests">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">The Six Tests</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Things you watch, not things you are told.</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Each one is runnable in a scheduled session. None of them requires a pilot store or a signature.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="evx">
      <div class="evx-item">
        <span class="evx-n">01</span>
        <h3>Pull the network in the middle, not at the start</h3>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Usually</span><p>The offline demo begins offline. The device is already disconnected, already in its offline state, already showing you the screen it was designed to show.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line is-do"><span class="evx-tag">Instead</span><p>Start the transaction connected. Scan two items, then take the network away between the basket and the payment. Then bring it back while a second sale is open.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Watching</span><p>Whether the sale completes at all, what the associate sees, and who owns the card authorization. Accepting a card offline is the payment provider's capability, not the POS vendor's. Ask which one you are being shown.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="evx-item">
        <span class="evx-n">02</span>
        <h3>Count a floor that is trading</h3>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Usually</span><p>The count is demonstrated on a fixed set of stock that nobody is buying, in a room where nothing moves.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line is-do"><span class="evx-tag">Instead</span><p>Ask what the count looks like at 2pm on a Saturday, with stock leaving the floor while the count is running.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Watching</span><p>Whether they admit to discrepancies. Stock moving during a count produces them, in every system ever built, and reconciling them is an operational job rather than a software feature. A vendor who says the count is simply correct is describing the demo condition.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="evx-item">
        <span class="evx-n">03</span>
        <h3>Hand the device to someone who has never seen it</h3>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Usually</span><p>The product is driven by the person who knows it best, narrating as they go, never pausing in the wrong place.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line is-do"><span class="evx-tag">Instead</span><p>Take the device yourself, with no script. Do a return against a gift receipt, then a split tender across a card and a gift card, then a price override.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Watching</span><p>Where you stop. Every place you have to ask a question is a place your seasonal staff will form a queue, and those three transactions are where most of a store's training time actually goes.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="evx-item">
        <span class="evx-n">04</span>
        <h3>Configure a market you do not trade in yet</h3>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Usually</span><p>You are shown a list of supported countries. A list is a statement of intent, not a demonstration.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line is-do"><span class="evx-tag">Instead</span><p>Name a market on your three-year plan and ask them to set it up while you watch: tax treatment, currency, receipt requirements, the local payment methods.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Watching</span><p>Whether it is configuration or a project. If the answer involves a scoping call, that is your answer, and it is not necessarily the wrong one. It just needs to be in the plan rather than in the pricing.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="evx-item">
        <span class="evx-n">05</span>
        <h3>Ask to watch a release reach a store</h3>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Usually</span><p>You are shown a roadmap. A roadmap tells you what a vendor intends; it tells you nothing about what happens on the day.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line is-do"><span class="evx-tag">Instead</span><p>Ask how a change reaches several thousand terminals, who decides when, whether a store can be left behind, and what the rollback looks like at 11am on Black Friday.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Watching</span><p>Whether upgrades are something that happens to you or something you schedule. This is the single question most likely to be missing from an RFP and most likely to shape the next five years.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="evx-item">
        <span class="evx-n">06</span>
        <h3>Ask how your data leaves</h3>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Usually</span><p>Nobody asks this during a selection, because it feels like planning the divorce during the engagement.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line is-do"><span class="evx-tag">Instead</span><p>Ask before you sign: which formats, what it costs, how long it takes, and whether transaction-level history comes with you or stays behind.</p></div>
        <div class="evx-line"><span class="evx-tag">Watching</span><p>How readily it is answered. You will never have more leverage than you do now, and the answer is a reasonable proxy for how the vendor behaves when a relationship is not going well.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ WHERE WE WOULD FAIL ============ -->
<section class="ac-purple sunk section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Turned Around</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Where Teamwork Commerce fails these tests.</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">If a testing guide from a vendor cannot be run against that vendor, it is marketing. Here is what we would have to tell you, in the room, on each of the above.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="evx-no">
      <div>
        <strong>Offline card acceptance is not ours</strong>
        <span>The app and its data sit on the device, so selling, counts, receiving and transfers continue through an outage and sync on reconnect. Whether a card can be authorized while the line is down depends on your payment provider, not on us.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Counting during trading produces discrepancies</strong>
        <span>Rolling counts run with the doors open, but stock keeps moving while you count, and what comes out needs reconciling. That is a logistics problem and no system removes it.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>We do not sell admission or food and beverage</strong>
        <span>For a museum or a venue, we run the shop, membership and donations. Ticketing stays with the system you already have.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Order routing does not price the shipment</strong>
        <span>Fill logic is a ranked rule stack on stock depth, node capacity and your own rules. It does not rate-shop carriers, route by distance, or calculate margin.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Reports are not self-serve</strong>
        <span>The 150+ reports ship out of the box, and anything beyond that library is built with you during implementation rather than assembled by your analyst on a Tuesday.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Promotions are not one cross-channel engine</strong>
        <span>Discounts, promotions and loyalty are configured in the POS. Carrying the same rules across to e-commerce and mobile is an integration, delivered through real-time connectors.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>We do not make the hardware</strong>
        <span>The RFID stack is native software with no middleware layer, but the readers and tags come from partners and are yours to choose. We are not an end-to-end hardware vendor.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>No EAS gate integration</strong>
        <span>Every unit is tracked by EPC from receiving through the sale. Tying that to exit gates is not something we do today.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

